the common ratio \\(r = \\frac{3}{5}\\), we can find the eighth term of the sequence. The \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence is given by \\(a_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}\\), where \\(a_1\\) is the first term and \\(n\\) is the term number. For the eighth term (\\(a_8\\)):\n\n\\[\na_8 = a_1 \\cdot r^{8-1} = \\frac{125}{9} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^7\n\\]\n\nWe need to calculate \\(\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^7\\):\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^7 = \\frac{3^7}{5^7} = \\frac{2187}{78125}\n\\]\n\nNow, we multiply \\(\\frac{125}{9}\\) by \\(\\frac{2187}{78125}\\):\n\n\\[\na_8 = \\frac{125}{9} \\cdot \\frac{2187}{78125} = \\frac{125 \\cdot 2187}{9 \\cdot 78125} = \\frac{273375}{703125}\n\\]\n\nWe simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{273375}{703125}\\). First, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 273375 and 703125. Using the Euclidean algorithm:\n\n\\[\n703125 = 273375 \\cdot 2 + 156375\n\\]\n\\[\n273375 = 156375 \\cdot 1 + 117000\n\\]\n\\[\n156375 = 117000 \\cdot 1 + 39375\n\\]\n\\[\n117000 = 39375 \\cdot 3 + 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the GCD is 39375. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 39375:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{273375 \\div 39375}{703125 \\div 39375} = \\frac{7}{18}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the eighth term of the sequence is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{18}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{7}{18}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:34"}
